 32 │ /**
 33 │  * The Transfers resource, under the {@code /api} prefix the security chain opens by name.
 34 │  *
 35 │  * <p>It holds no repository — the boundary {@code ModuleBoundariesHoldTest} enforces — and
 36 │  * no rule of its own: what a request may say is declared as constraints on
 37 │  * {@link CreateTransferRequest}, and every refusal a Transfer can meet is raised where the
 38 │  * facts to decide it are, under the lock. What this class adds is the last step, below:
 39 │  * which status code each of those refusals is worth.
 40 │  *
 41 │  * <p><b>The refusal handlers sit on the controller rather than in a {@code
 42 │  * @ControllerAdvice}</b>, where they are reached by the structure of the resolver's lookup.
 43 │  * An advice would instead have to out-order {@code ProblemDocumentAdvice}, whose handler for
 44 │  * {@code Exception} would otherwise answer every refusal below as a {@code 500} (design
 45 │  * decision 14).
 46 │  *
 47 │  * <p>The path is written out again in the tests that call it, rather than read from the
 48 │  * constant below, on {@code AccountController}'s reasoning: a renamed path is a breaking
 49 │  * change for the generated frontend types, and a test reading the same constant as the
 50 │  * mapping would follow the rename silently instead of failing on it.
